00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.380
From the NFL's deflategate and head trauma controversies to the MLB's doping scandals

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:12.400
and match-fixing that dates back to 1919,

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.570
one thing is pretty clear when it comes to

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:20.500
professional sports, sometimes the spirit
of competition gets lost in the money and fame.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.260
And esports is no exception.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.440
Back in 2009, esports was at best a buzzword in the west.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:33.340
But on the other side of the world, the industry was already an established hub of opportunity.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.060
You need to see this.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:39.440
With the rise of Starcraft's popularity in
Korea, a significant number of illegal

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:43.580
gambling websites sprung up as a way for
people to capitalize on the number of

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:46.580
yearly competitions that took place
within the country.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.700
By 2010, several brokers representing these sites

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.720
used their connection with the pro league circuit

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.060
to entice players to throw matches.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.720
The money involved made it an

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:59.320
easy sell and more than ten players
including some of the game's most

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:06.160
prominent members, like CJ Entus' sAviOr
and STX Soul's Hwasin participated in the scandal.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:20.800
The scandal turned the esports world upside down.
Even the mainstream media who had little

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:25.400
to no interest in esports in 2010 wrote
about the scandal comparing it to the

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:29.690
1919 Black Sox scandal in which eight
members of the Chicago White Sox

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:33.720
baseball team were found to have
deliberately lost games.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.800
By 2011, a new title

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:40.400
League of Legends was gaining
popularity, especially in the West.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.959
reaching a hundred and eighty eight
thousand viewers on our stream

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:48.860
it's ridiculous! That..I..we were expecting
something like that for the grand finals maybe.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.000
But much like its RTS predecessor,
the games growth

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:57.049
meant that its integrity would be put to
the test. Then, in 2012, League of Legends

00:02:57.049 --> 00:02:59.900
experienced its first major scandal.

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:03.460
Team Curse here and we just want to apologize to MLG and the fans.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:05.420
Team Dignitas and Team Curse NA

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:08.600
privately agreed to play out the
first match as an All Random All Mid

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:13.400
ahead of the MLG Summer Championships
grand finals and their collusion cost

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.100
them forty thousand dollars in winnings.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.760
The interesting thing about this is that

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:23.480
Dignitas has their seating relied on
this game, if they win this series they'd

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:26.569
move into second place and face a
different team in the first round they'd

00:03:26.569 --> 00:03:28.640
dodged out of Team Dynamic.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:30.780
What is going on?!

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.700
In the same year, during the World Championships,

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:36.859
a critical design flaw
with the stage allowed players who

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:41.269
looked up at the venue's main screen to
catch glimpses of the enemy's mini-map.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.140
The screens are behind the players. So, there's always a chance of cheating.

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:48.000
Though the accusations initially began online,

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.319
Riot's internal
investigation found that five instances

00:03:51.319 --> 00:03:55.940
of the players looking up at the
jumbotron took place. The most severe of

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:59.660
which occurred during a match between
Korea's Azubu Frost and the North

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:02.020
America's Team SoloMid.

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:05.540
Woong, the AD
carry for Azubu Frost was caught looking

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:09.290
at the venue screen and the team
subsequently modified their gameplay

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:14.940
based on the information gathered.
Effectively nullifying TSM's level one strategy.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:19.540
The first game was kind of
disappointing to me mainly because they did cheat.

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:23.960
Though some people questioned
exactly how advantageous the screen

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:30.300
watching was, the community was furious
and esports' integrity was once again under the microscope

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.760
In 2013, esports as
a whole took a giant leap forward.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.280
League of Legends hosted the most viewed
esports event of the year,

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:43.500
with 32 million unique
viewers, an exponential growth from 2012's

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:50.620
8.2 million. And Dota 2's The International
tournament handed out over 2.8 million dollars in prize money.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:54.320
But as the scene
attracted more views and higher payouts,

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.660
it also attracted more people.

00:04:56.660 --> 00:05:00.520
Some of which did not have the industry's best interests at heart.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:09.800
In 2013, Alexei "Solo" Berezin famously threw a dota 2 match for 322 dollars

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:11.540
They just run in there to die!

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:14.760
and a rogue employee at ESEA uploaded malware

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.780
into the company's software
allowing him to farm bitcoins

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:22.950
unbeknownst to the software's users. The
malware initially began as an experiment

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:28.110
run by ESEA on two of their own admins
computers for the purposes of testing

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:32.550
Bitcoin integration for their clients,
but the company eventually decided to

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:37.139
scrap the project. Though the project was
scrapped, one of the employees involved

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:41.840
in the beta tests decided to use the
code to mine bitcoins for personal gain.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:47.300
That's..that's unreal. Absolutely is, I can't believe it. I think most people couldn't.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:52.960
due to the excessive strain that mining put on
users computers, many ESEA users were

00:05:52.979 --> 00:05:56.850
left with fried video cards and
astronomical electricity bills.

00:05:56.850 --> 00:06:01.050
In the end, a class-action lawsuit saw
ESEA hit with a 1 million dollar fine

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:05.580
for a combined total gain of just over
3700 dollars.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.780
I've noticed that, they kind of messed themselves up with the money situation.

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:14.420
In 2014, the entire International esports
Federation was exposed for having a

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:18.870
male's only rule that excluded females
